course: myths
lesson: 16
title: Water, deserts and the shape of the land
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: "Mate, in Australia the toilet flushes the OTHER way round. It's the Coriolis effect, it's science!" Your cousin is back from Sydney and he says it like a man who has seen the edge of the world. Add quicksand that swallows explorers whole, glass that slowly drips down old church windows, and diamonds squeezed out of lumps of coal, and you have the best playground geography there is.
explain: The trouble is that each of these stories starts from something real and then stretches it too far. The Coriolis effect is real, but it only steers huge things like hurricanes; a toilet bowl is far too small, so its swirl comes from the shape of the bowl and the way the water rushes in.
explain: Many arguments are really about words. "Biggest desert" depends on what a desert is (a place with very little rain or snow, so frozen Antarctica counts), and "tallest mountain" depends on whether you measure from the sea or from the mountain's own foot on the sea floor.
explain: The best weapon is a simple test. Pull the plug in your sink five times, balance an egg on an ordinary Tuesday, leave salty water in the sun, stir cornflour and water into gloop: the world answers for itself.
explain: And when your friend clings to his story, you don't need to win; say what is real in it, then offer to test it together.
fact: The Coriolis effect is real and makes hurricanes spin opposite ways in the north and south of the world, but it is far too weak to decide which way a sink or toilet drains.
fact: A desert is a place with very little rain or snow; the biggest desert is Antarctica, and the Sahara is the biggest hot desert.
fact: Most of the world's deserts are rock, gravel, mountains or ice; only about a fifth to a quarter of the Sahara is sand dunes.
fact: Mount Everest has the highest peak above sea level, 8,849 metres; measured from its base on the sea floor, Mauna Kea in Hawaii is taller, over 10,000 metres, though most of it is hidden under the sea.
fact: Glass is a solid; old windows are uneven because old glass was made uneven and often fitted thick side down, and Roman glass 2,000 years old has not sagged.
fact: Diamonds and coal are both made of carbon, but most diamonds formed deep in the Earth long before the land plants that made coal existed.
fact: The Nile is usually listed as the longest river, but some studies say the Amazon, because experts disagree about where each river starts; the Amazon carries by far the most water.
fact: The sea is salty because rain wears tiny bits of minerals from rocks and rivers carry them to the sea; the water evaporates (dries up into the air) and the salt stays behind.
fact: Quicksand can trap your legs, but a person floats in it and does not sink over their head; you get free by moving slowly and leaning back.
fact: Earthquakes start kilometres underground, and the weather has no effect on them; there is no such thing as "earthquake weather".
fact: With patience you can balance an egg on its end on any day of the year; the "only on the equinox" story was spread by a newspaper story in 1945.
exercise: Your cousin swears toilets in Australia flush the other way "because of the Coriolis effect". What is true? | The effect is real for hurricanes but far too weak for a toilet | The effect pulls all water south below the equator | Australian toilets are built backwards on purpose | The effect only works on salty water
exercise: So what decides which way your sink swirls when you pull the plug? | The shape of the sink and how the water moved in | Which half of the world, north or south, you live in | The time of day or night when you pull out the plug | Whether the water from the tap is hot or cold that day
exercise: Quiz master: "Biggest desert in the world? The Sahara!" What's the better answer? | Antarctica, because a desert means very little rain or snow | The Sahara, because it has the most sand | The Gobi, because it is the coldest and driest of all deserts | The Pacific, because nothing grows there
exercise: Then what is the Sahara the biggest of? | The biggest hot desert | The biggest desert of any kind | The biggest sand dune | The biggest dry lake
exercise: Daisy after an adventure film: "Deserts are all sand dunes." What are most deserts really like? | Mostly rock, gravel, mountains or ice | Nearly all deep, soft sand from end to end | Sand lying on top of a hidden underground sea | Sand dunes and palm trees almost everywhere
exercise: Seb bets his crisps that Everest is the tallest mountain. What do you tell him? | Highest above sea level, yes; from base to top Mauna Kea is taller | Yes, nothing is taller however you measure | No, Ben Nevis is taller from its base | No, Everest has shrunk below K2
exercise: Why can Mauna Kea be "taller" than Everest? | Most of it is hidden under the sea | It keeps growing a metre every year | It is measured from the Moon | Its snow cap adds a kilometre
exercise: Grace: "Glass is a really slow liquid, that's why old windows are thick at the bottom." What's the real reason? | Old glass was made uneven and often fitted thick side down | The glass has slowly dripped down the pane over hundreds of years | Sunlight melts the top of the window slowly | Rain washes glass down the pane each winter
exercise: Thomas: "Diamonds are just squashed coal." What is true? | Both are carbon, but most diamonds are older than coal | Every diamond was once a lump of coal deep in the ground | Coal is made from crushed diamonds | Diamonds are made of glass, not carbon
exercise: Nina: "The Amazon is the longest river, full stop." What's the honest answer? | Usually the Nile is listed, but experts argue about the sources | The Amazon wins by thousands of kilometres, and all experts agree | The Mississippi is the longest | Nobody has ever measured either river
exercise: Which river carries by far the most water? | The Amazon | The Nile | The Thames | The Danube
exercise: Rhys, giggling: "The sea's salty cos of fish wee!" Where does the salt really come from? | Minerals worn from rocks by rain and carried by rivers | Fish and whales making salt in their bodies and letting it out | Salt mines at the bottom of the sea | Salty rain falling only on the oceans
exercise: Lena: "Quicksand sucks you under and drowns you!" What really happens? | It can trap your legs, but you float and don't sink under | It pulls you down faster and faster the harder you stand still | It swallows people in a few seconds | It only lets go if someone pulls very hard
exercise: Stuck in quicksand to your knees. What helps? | Move slowly and lean back | Kick hard and fast | Stand perfectly still for an hour | Jump up and down to break free
exercise: Alfie's uncle: "Hot, still day, proper earthquake weather!" What is true? | Earthquakes start deep underground and weather can't affect them | Hot days soften the rock under the ground and so set off big quakes | Earthquakes only happen when there is no wind | Cold weather freezes faults and stops quakes
exercise: Ellie's chain message: "You can stand an egg on its end ONLY on the spring equinox!" What is true? | With patience you can do it any day of the year | Only on the equinox, because of the Sun's special pull | Only on the equinox, and only exactly at midnight | Never on any day, eggs are the wrong shape to stand
test: You pull the plug in your sink five times and it swirls clockwise twice and anticlockwise three times. What does that show? | The swirl depends on the sink and water, not the half of the world | Your house is exactly on the equator | The Coriolis effect changes every minute | Your sink is broken and needs fixing
test: "omg my cousin literally showed me a video of an Aussie toilet going backwards, so it's TRUE" What's wrong with that proof? | Toilets go either way anywhere; one video doesn't show the cause | Nothing, a video always proves the cause, so it must be totally true | Videos from Australia are filmed upside down | Only the cousin's toilet works that way
test: A cartoon explorer crosses the Antarctic ice and says "Phew, glad we're out of the desert!" when he reaches it. What's wrong? | Antarctica is itself a desert, the biggest one | Antarctica is really a frozen sea, not land at all | Deserts can only be hot places, so ice can't be one | Nothing, Antarctica gets heaps of rain and snow
test: Your mate says, "Shut up, a desert's hot and sandy, that's what the word means." How do you argue wisely? | Look up the meaning together: little rain or snow | Shout until he gives in | Tell him he's thick and walk off | Agree with him to keep the peace, even though it's wrong
test: What would happen if you left a bowl of seawater on a sunny windowsill for a week? | The water would go and leave salt behind | The salt would go off and leave fresh water | The water would slowly turn into a block of ice | Nothing, because seawater never evaporates
test: In a film, quicksand closes over the hero's hat and he's gone. What's the true version? | People float in quicksand, so it can't pull you under | Quicksand really does swallow people whole within seconds | Only heavy people sink all the way | Quicksand works like a whirlpool
test: Why does "Roman glass hasn't sagged in 2,000 years" beat the dripping-window story? | If glass flowed, very old glass would have sagged far more | Romans used special glass that cannot melt | Glass only drips in churches | Roman glass was always kept in the fridge
test: Thomas won't give up: "But they're BOTH carbon, so I'm right!" Best reply? | Sharing an ingredient doesn't mean one turned into the other | Then pencils must be made of diamonds | You're right, any carbon always turns into diamond in the end | Coal and diamonds are not carbon at all
test: "which is longer nile or amazon, just tell me the answer" What's the honest reply? | Usually the Nile, but experts disagree about where they start | The Amazon, everyone agrees | The Nile, everyone agrees | They are exactly the same length
test: Alfie's uncle remembers a big quake on a hot day. Why doesn't that prove "earthquake weather"? | One hot day is a coincidence; quakes come on every kind of day | It proves it, one example is enough | Quakes only happen in summer anyway | The hot weather warms the rock deep down and that makes it crack
test: The Weekly Wonder says eggs stand up only on 20 March. What's the smartest move? | Try to balance one today and see | Wait till March before believing anything | Trust it, newspapers are always right | Ask a friend who heard it too
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the largest desert upon the Earth? | It is Antarctica, a desert being defined by scant precipitation | It is the Sahara, a desert being defined by the presence of sand | It is the Gobi, a desert being defined by low temperature | It is Arabia, a desert being defined by the absence of rivers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the direction of rotation of water draining from a domestic basin is not determined by the Coriolis effect. | The effect is far too weak at so small a scale | The effect operates solely upon salt water | The effect is reversed within enclosed buildings | The effect ceases in the hours of darkness
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the salinity of the oceans. | Minerals eroded from rock are conveyed by rivers and remain upon evaporation | Salinity is produced by the excretions of marine animals | Salinity originates exclusively from saline rainfall | Salinity is generated by the action of the tides
exam: It is given that Mount Everest attains 8,849 metres above sea level, whereas Mauna Kea exceeds 10,000 metres when measured from its base upon the sea floor. Select the most accurate conclusion. | The designation of the tallest mountain depends upon the method of measurement | Everest is the tallest by every method of measurement | Mauna Kea is the highest above sea level | Neither summit has been measured with accuracy
